===Etymology===
{{multiple images
|direction = vertical
|image1 = Baddha utthita parsvakonasana.jpg
|caption1 = A man adopting the ''baddha utthita parsvakonasana'' [[asana]] or [[yoga]] stance ''(sense 1)''
|image2 = Final 2013 Fencing WCH FMS-IN t200208.jpg
|caption2 = Italy's {{w|Arianna Errigo}} ''(left)'' and Germany’s {{w|Carolin Golubytskyi}} taking [[fencing#Noun|fencing]] stances ''(sense 1)'' during the {{w|2013 World Fencing Championships}} in [[Budapest]], [[Hungary]]
|image3 = Edinburgh - Taxis at Station.jpg
|caption3 = A [[taxi#Noun|taxi]] stance ''(sense 4)'' at {{w|Edinburgh Waverley railway station}}, [[Scotland]], UK
}}

{{root|en|ine-pro|*steh₂-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|staunce||place to stand; battle station; position; standing in society; circumstance, situation; stanchion}}, from {{der|en|fro|estance||predicament; situation; sojourn, stay}}<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=staunce|pos=''n.''(2)|noformat=yes|id=MED42697|accessdate=30 September 2018}}</ref> (compare modern {{cog|fr|stance||stanza; position one stands in when golfing}}), from {{der|en|it|stanza||room, standing place; stanza}},<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=''n.''{{sup|2}}|noformat=yes|id=188941|date=1915|nodot=yes}}; {{R:Lexico}}</ref> from {{der|en|la-vul|*stantia}}, from {{der|en|la|stō||to stand; to remain, stay}}, ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*steh₂-||to stand (up)}}. The word is cognate with {{cog|es|estante||shelf}} and a {{doublet|en|stanza|nocap=1}}.

The verb is derived from the noun.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=v|id=188942|date=1915}}</ref>

Compare typologically {{cog|cs|postoj||stance (the way of holding a body); stance (point of view)}} (cognate via PIE). Also see {{m|en|position}}, {{m|en|posture}}.

